On Monday, August 31, 2015 at 10:46:50 PM UTC+2, Bob Carpenter wrote:<blockquote class="gmail_quote" style="margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex">
<br>&gt; On Aug 31, 2015, at 8:51 AM, Bruno Nicenboim &lt;<a rel="nofollow">bruno.n...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:
<br>&gt; 
<br>&gt; 
<br>&gt; Hi, I&#39;m back with my shifted-lognormal hierarchical model (this is my previous post), I
<br>&gt; started to increase the complexity of the model, and I started to notice
<br>&gt; problems in convergence. So I simplified everything again to check it.
<br>&gt; Basically I&#39;m trying to fit a model where reading times (rt) are assumed to be
<br>&gt; produced by a shifted lognormal distribution. I&#39;m assuming a general shift, a 
<br>&gt; by-subject adjustment coming from a normal distribution centered in 0, an
<br>&gt; intercept for the lognormal part (alpha), a fixed effect (beta), and 
<br>&gt; by-subjects random intercept and slopes centered in 0 that they are somewhat
<br>&gt; correlated: (the complete code is in shifted_log_simple.stan)
<br>&gt; 
<br>&gt; 
<br>&gt;  transformed parameters{
<br>&gt;  (...)
<br>&gt;  for (i in 1:N_obs){
<br>&gt;     mu[i] &lt;- alpha  + x_betas[i] * beta +   
<br>&gt;           x_u[i] * u[subj[i]];
<br>&gt;     psi[i] &lt;-  shift + shift_u[subj[i]]; //
<br>&gt;   }
<br>&gt; }
<br>&gt; 
<br>&gt; 
<br>&gt; model {
<br>&gt;  (...)
<br>&gt;      (rt - psi) ~ lognormal(mu, sigma);
<br>&gt; }
<br>&gt; 
<br>&gt; 
<br>&gt; 
<br>&gt; So the same parameters that don&#39;t get an Rhat of 1 in the complex mode, here
<br>&gt; they are around 1.01-1.02 (while the other params are more in the
<br>&gt; 1.00something).
<br>&gt; 
<br>&gt; These parameters are the ones that are in a different scale than the rest:
<br>&gt; alpha (the intercept of fixed effects) has an estimate around 5 (I simulate
<br>&gt; this but that&#39;s also the case in &quot;real life&quot;), the intercept for shift is
<br>&gt; around 200, while the adjustment for shift for participants is between 0-200.
<br>&gt; (The other parameters are below 1). I checked the get_sampler_params, and I
<br>&gt; noticed that Max treedepth reaches 11 and n_divergent has a Max of 1, which I
<br>&gt; understand from the manual is something bad.
<br>&gt; 
<br>&gt; So from reading previous answers in the forum, I understand that there are two
<br>&gt; possibilities: either to decrease the stepsize and increase the treedepth; or
<br>&gt; to normalize the parameters to be near 1. I&#39;m trying to avoid the first
<br>&gt; possibility just because it&#39;s extremely slow. 
<br>&gt; 
<br>&gt; Regarding the second option, one problem that I face is that the
<br>&gt; initialization fails unless I set init=0.
<br>
<br>Do you mean throws exceptions then quits?  This is usually a sign that your
<br>constraints don&#39;t match the support of your model, which can also cause
<br>all sorts of problems downstream with divergent iterations (going out of support
<br>is a kind of divergence).
<br>
<br>Which of these models has this problem?  I&#39;m afraid I don&#39;t have time to
<br>read three models and diff them in my head, but I can check over one
<br>for constraints matching support.
<br>
<br></blockquote>
